sm80 Marlin repack: stage the per-layer transients through one grow-only
buffer (#27; allocation hygiene — the wedge theory below was DISPROVEN).

History, kept honest: this patch was written against the theory that VMM
map/unmap churn during Marlin repack wedged ahnguyen17's CMP 170HX (Xid 31,
drifting faulting frames, wedge-until-reboot). The real root cause was
found by ahnguyen17 afterwards and is not vLLM's at all: their CMP unlock
driver registered the top reserved framebuffer region — which backs
WPR2/GSP firmware — into the page allocator; it sorts last, so it is handed
out only near full occupancy, which produced exactly the occupancy
dependence and frame drift this header once attributed to churn. With the
unlocker fixed, the stock un-staged repack path boots and serves clean on
the same card (issue #27, final comments). Do NOT cite this patch as
evidence of an sm80 Marlin bug; stock-map sm80 (A100s) never had this
failure mode.

What survives, and why the patch stays: staging is measurably better
allocation behavior during load. On the 170HX A/B (w8a16 target, full
serving session, 8 streams): repack window 44.9 s against the CPU
fallback's 403.7 s, engine ready ~90 s against ~540 s, decode identical to
three decimals, outputs bit-exact end to end, buffer cost inside noise
(≤0.15 GiB there). ahnguyen17 promoted it to their production after the
driver fix on those grounds.

This is the GPU-resident version of that workaround: pad+contiguous land in
ONE persistent, grow-only staging buffer per device, and the source tensor
is dropped before the repack output allocates. Per-layer allocation traffic
falls from two transients + one output to just the output; the staging
buffer reallocates only when a larger shape class first appears (a handful
of times per load, instead of hundreds of alloc/free pairs).

Bit-exactness is by construction — the repack kernel receives identical
bytes — and verified on the 3090 across the checkpoint's real shape ladder
(w4 and w8, padded and unpadded, contiguous and strided sources, buffer
reuse and regrowth): kernel inputs and outputs torch.equal in all cases.

Costs and gating: the buffer stays resident after load — up to ~1.2 GiB on
this stack, because the quantized embedding/lm_head whales set its size.
That is why it defaults ON only for compute capability 8.0 exactly (the
card class that ships 40-64 GiB, where 1.2 GiB is cheap and load time is
9x), and OFF everywhere else. Override with VLLM_MARLIN_REPACK_STAGED=0/1.
ahnguyen17's CPU-fallback patch remains the zero-VRAM alternative.

Status: validated on the 170HX (4 boot cycles + a full serving session,
bit-exact, 0 Xid) and in production there — but as load-time hygiene, not
as the wedge fix this header originally proposed; see the history above.

